SUMMARY:A Democratic View of Geopolitics
DESCRIPTION: \nIn the map of the world we have long known\, the US and Western powers stand at one pole\, with Russia and China at the other: ideological\, institutional\, intentional. \nThis discussion\, based on the book The De-democratisation Project\, argues that seismic shifts in the world\, the genocide in Gaza\, Russia’s war on Ukraine\, the US-Israel war on Iran\, cannot be understood through the outmoded West-versus-Rest lens. The true crisis of our time\, it suggests\, is a global attempt to undo the hard-won consensus that democracy and human rights are universal. The conversation imagines a different map altogether: one built on solidarity\, and the shared aspiration of democracy. \nIn collaboration with: \n \n\n\nHuman Rights and the Idea of Solidarity Without Polarity\nRSVP here (https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeLO1O6tuDXcHTy0QsD0S2git1PJIy72WWmI_AG-hZopVSD7g/viewform?usp=publish-editor)\n*Speakers*: Kavita Krishnan\, Arvind Narrain\n*Event Categories*: Politics
URL:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/event/a-democratic-view-of-geopolitics/
LOCATION:Bangalore International Centre\, 7\, 4th Main Road\, Domlur II Stage\, Bangalore\, Karnataka\, 560071\, India
CATEGORIES:Politics
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://bangaloreinternationalcentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Thumbnail-12-1.jpg
END:VEVENT
